WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.130
On this IC Command Board, the training warehouse call is already open.

00:00:04.619 --> 00:00:08.319
Select Tactical Maps to review additional maps for this incident.

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.250
The count is zero, and the empty state explains that a map can

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:14.260
represent an area of operation.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:18.250
Choose Add in this section to begin a named reference map.

00:00:18.459 --> 00:00:22.249
New Tactical Map provides Name, Description and Expires.

00:00:22.689 --> 00:00:25.559
Before a name is entered, Add is unavailable.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:32.280
Read the expiry choices: None, twelve hours, one day, two days or seven days.

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:36.389
Decide what the map should identify and how long that reference should remain

00:00:36.389 --> 00:00:38.090
relevant to your department’s work.

00:00:38.269 --> 00:00:41.009
Enter a name that another operator can recognize.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:44.540
Here it is Training Only, West gate coordination.

00:00:44.979 --> 00:00:48.200
The Description explains that this is a fictional reference area

00:00:48.229 --> 00:00:49.630
for the training staging lane.

00:00:50.059 --> 00:00:54.639
The twelve-hour choice has been selected, and the form displays a calculated expiry.

00:00:55.049 --> 00:00:58.850
Review the entered description and displayed time before choosing Add.

00:00:59.039 --> 00:01:02.099
The board now shows Tactical Maps with a count of one.

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:06.869
The card contains the saved name, description and an Expires label.

00:01:07.359 --> 00:01:11.510
Compare that readback with the prepared form: this recording shows different

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:13.170
clock times in those two views.

00:01:13.579 --> 00:01:17.499
Verify the intended expiry in your department before relying on it.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:21.330
The named card gives the next operator a clear purpose to check

00:01:21.439 --> 00:01:22.830
when opening this reference.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.309
Use the pencil button on the Staging lane to open its settings,

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:28.800
then scroll to Attached Map.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:32.739
None and the named West gate coordination map are available.

00:01:33.189 --> 00:01:36.869
Select the intended map and check the selected state before saving.

00:01:37.189 --> 00:01:40.080
This associates that reference with the lane you are editing.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:43.769
Keep the lane identity and the map’s name clear when several

00:01:43.769 --> 00:01:45.259
areas are being coordinated.

00:01:45.479 --> 00:01:47.640
Choose Save in the lane settings.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:53.130
After a fresh page load, reopen the same Staging lane and return to Attached Map.

00:01:53.519 --> 00:01:55.599
The named map remains selected here.

00:01:55.969 --> 00:01:58.540
This readback confirms the saved association.

00:01:59.009 --> 00:02:02.499
Check the selected map whenever a lane’s purpose or working area

00:02:02.499 --> 00:02:06.120
changes, so the next operator can find the intended reference.

00:02:06.269 --> 00:02:08.259
Return to Tactical Maps on the board.

00:02:08.779 --> 00:02:13.310
The count of one, the saved name, Description and Expires label are still

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.260
present after the fresh navigation.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:17.319
Read the card before opening it.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.700
A short, specific description helps distinguish a staging reference from

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:26.469
another team’s search area, patrol sector or industrial exercise location.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:28.810
Open the card and check the map’s header.

00:02:29.209 --> 00:02:33.279
It shows the same West gate coordination name, with the training street map and

00:02:33.279 --> 00:02:35.610
Squad Three’s Search Division label below.

00:02:36.079 --> 00:02:39.640
Use that identity check when sharing references with another operator.

00:02:40.169 --> 00:02:43.650
Named areas can support community paramedicine or mental health

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:47.779
coordination, search and rescue, security companies, wildland

00:02:47.779 --> 00:02:52.570
contractors, industrial response teams, and fire or EMS departments.

00:02:53.039 --> 00:02:56.369
Choose names and descriptions that explain your own team’s work.
